      {
        "id": "p48-q34-a",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "a",
        "prompt": "Show that $\\sin 3x=3\\sin x-4\\sin^3 x$.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "Expand $\\sin(2x+x)$ using the sine addition formula, then use the double-angle identities and $\\cos^2x=1-\\sin^2x$ to obtain $\\sin3x=3\\sin x-4\\sin^3x$.",
          "worked": "$$\\begin{align*}\\sin3x&=\\sin(2x+x)\\\\\\sin3x&=\\sin2x\\cos x+\\cos2x\\sin x\\\\\\sin3x&=2\\sin x\\cos^2x+(1-2\\sin^2x)\\sin x\\\\\\sin3x&=2\\sin x(1-\\sin^2x)+\\sin x-2\\sin^3x\\\\\\sin3x&=3\\sin x-4\\sin^3x\\end{align*}$$",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 44
      },
      {
        "id": "p48-q34-b",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "b",
        "prompt": "Hence, solve $\\sin3x=\\sin2x$ for $0^\\circ\\le x\\le360^\\circ$.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": {
            "format": "maths-editor-document-v1",
            "version": 1,
            "blocks": [
              {
                "id": "p48-q34-b-short-result",
                "type": "paragraph",
                "fontSize": null,
                "align": "left",
                "spaceBefore": 0,
                "spaceAfter": 2,
                "lineHeight": 1.4,
                "indent": 0,
                "inlines": [
                  {
                    "type": "math",
                    "latex": "x=0^\\circ,36^\\circ,108^\\circ,180^\\circ,252^\\circ,324^\\circ,360^\\circ",
                    "display": false
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "id": "p48-q34-b-short-method",
                "type": "paragraph",
                "fontSize": null,
                "align": "left",
                "spaceBefore": 0,
                "spaceAfter": 2,
                "lineHeight": 1.4,
                "indent": 0,
                "inlines": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Use part (a) and the double-angle formula to factor as ",
                    "marks": []
                  },
                  {
                    "type": "math",
                    "latex": "\\sin x(4\\cos^2x-2\\cos x-1)=0",
                    "display": false
                  },
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": ", then solve both factors.",
                    "marks": []
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          "worked": "Using part (a) and $\\sin2x=2\\sin x\\cos x$,\n$$\\begin{align*}3\\sin x-4\\sin^3x&=2\\sin x\\cos x\\\\\\sin x(3-4\\sin^2x-2\\cos x)&=0\\\\\\sin x\\bigl(3-4(1-\\cos^2x)-2\\cos x\\bigr)&=0\\\\\\sin x(4\\cos^2x-2\\cos x-1)&=0\\end{align*}$$\nSolve each factor separately.\n$$\\begin{align*}\\sin x&=0\\\\x&=0^\\circ,180^\\circ,360^\\circ\\end{align*}$$\nFor the other factor,\n$$\\begin{align*}4\\cos^2x-2\\cos x-1&=0\\\\\\cos x&=\\frac{2\\pm\\sqrt{4+16}}{8}\\\\\\cos x&=\\frac{1\\pm\\sqrt5}{4}\\\\x&=36^\\circ,108^\\circ,252^\\circ,324^\\circ\\end{align*}$$\nBoth factors give solutions of the original equation; no factor was cancelled and no squaring was used.\n$$x=0^\\circ,36^\\circ,108^\\circ,180^\\circ,252^\\circ,324^\\circ,360^\\circ.$$",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 134
      }
    ]
